WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.080
 You know, I think computer security is the most exciting part of computing right now.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.460
 Because it has something that nothing else has.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.120
 It has an adversary relationship.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.920
 I mean, if you do graphics or operating systems or anything, there's no one trying to thwart

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:23.920
 you at every turn.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.040
 And that's what you have in security.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.160
 That's what makes it exciting and interesting.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.820
 And that's what makes it something that's forever changing and involves psychology and

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:39.600
 economics and computing and law and policy and so many things.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.000
 So I think it's a great area to be in, to work in.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.580
 I think it's not going away, right?

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:50.600
 As long as we have adversaries, as long as we have human beings and ne'er-do-wells and

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.380
 evildoers, we're going to need security.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.720
 So it's always going to be like that.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.420
 You know, preparing is interesting.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.520
 In a lot of ways, security is a mindset.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.920
 It's a way of thinking about the world.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.960
 And if you think about the traditional definition of a hacker, someone who sort of cobbles stuff

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.040
 together, you hack this tool and it works and you put this piece together and this here

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.960
 and that and it all works and it's a great hack.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:17.960
 But I'm a security guy.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.580
 I'm going to say, well, turn this like that and it doesn't work anymore.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.080
 And you'll say, well, don't do that.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.840
 And I'll say, well, no, no, no, I'm the attacker.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.000
 I get to do that.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.880
 I get to do that whenever I want.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.360
 I get to do it at the most inopportune time.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.800
 I get to do that in a way that makes your system fail as badly as possible.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.280
 And you have to think that way.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:43.200
 Not about how to build something, not how to make it work, but how to make it fail and

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.120
 how to make it fail in precisely the right way to do precisely the right sort of damage.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:50.680
 And that's a way of thinking.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:55.920
 I mean, there are some people who go through their lives looking at systems and figuring

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.120
 out, oh, I can break that.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.440
 Oh, here's how to break that.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.760
 And you walk into a store and you see the purchasing system.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:02.760
 Oh, I can steal something.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:03.760
 Here's how.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:04.760
 You walk into a voting booth.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.380
 Oh, I can sort of defeat this.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.380
 Here's how.

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:13.160
 You might not do it because, of course, that'd be illegal, but you think that way.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.320
 And that mindset, I think, is essential for security.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:22.820
 Once you have that mindset, then it's a matter of just learning the domain, right?

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:29.000
 Learning the systems and whether it's a self-driving car or a voting system or a medical device.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.680
 It's going to be embedded code, interacting with the real world in a way that involves

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.120
 people and society.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.760
 And I can teach all that.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.320
 You can learn all that.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.320
 So I remember a class in security.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:43.480
 I forget who did this.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:49.460
 One of the assignments was, "Come in tomorrow and write down the first thousand digits of

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.460
 pi."

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:54.120
 Okay, so two things about this test.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.680
 One, you can't memorize a thousand digits of pi.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.320
 You have to cheat.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.280
 And actually, the students were expected to cheat.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:07.440
 But if they were court cheating, they would fail.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:08.440
 That's interesting, right?

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.600
 That teaches that mindset.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.660
 It allows you to think outside the box.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.160
 How am I going to do this?

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.280
 And there are lots of ways people cheated.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:25.060
 And I sort of urge people watching to go Google this and to look at some of the stuff written.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:29.080
 It's a great way of trying to stimulate the mindset.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.200
 Can you teach it formally?

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.200
 I don't know.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.000
 It's kind of like it's a way of thinking.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.640
 And I think the more security classes you take, the more you exercise that mindset.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.120
 A lot of the hacker conferences will have capture the flag contests.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:49.800
 I remember an early one where they had to build their own private network to cut down

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:55.360
 on both network latency and federal violations.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.640
 That's why you do it.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.960
 But you're going to learn a lot by breaking other people's systems.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:06.560
 And yeah, that's probably going to involve illegal activity.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.480
 And agreed, this isn't the best way, or maybe it is the best way, it's not the most socially

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:12.480
 acceptable way.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:19.320
 But here we have this clash between the tech imperative and what society wants.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.240
 So many of our systems are black boxes.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:30.600
 You can go and try to hack this, your smartphone or your computer, and there's a lot of stuff

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.100
 you can learn.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:35.840
 But really it's going to be more fun if you can hack somebody else's cell phone or somebody

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:37.120
 else's computer.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.920
 I want it to be open-ended.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:42.880
 I want it to follow whatever it is you're interested in.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.880
 The neat thing about security is it can go wherever you want.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:50.000
 There are so many different sub-disciplines.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:58.200
 I'm often asked, "Should I study forensics or cryptography or network security or protocols

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.800
 or embedded devices or scada systems?"

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.800
 Study what you want.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.480
 Whatever interests you, follow that.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:14.520
 Because really what you're learning is how to think like a security expert.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:18.300
 And honestly, if you get a job and they make you do VPNs, you can pick up VPNs.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:19.440
 That's easy.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:20.760
 It's the way to think.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.320
 So do what you want.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:29.040
 And what we're learning right now is that demand is greatly outstripping supply.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:35.040
 That people with expertise in security have a guaranteed career because there is such

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.120
 a demand for it and there is such a lack of supply.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:44.180
 Have you written any of your books aimed at those pre-computer science students or early

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:47.320
 computer science students that would be a good read?

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:50.520
 I tend to write my books for general audience.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:55.520
 So I think of my parents, my friends.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.540
 So computer experts, yes, but really for a more general audience.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:04.600
 So going back to something like Secrets and Lies I wrote in 2000, it's about how network

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:05.600
 security works.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:11.000
 I had 15 years out of date, but it's still a good introduction on the basic concepts

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.920
 of how to think about security.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:19.400
 Later cryptography engineering, how to engineer crypto systems.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:25.420
 My book, Liars and Outliers, how to think about security as a way to enable trust.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:32.120
 Very non-technical, but very much here's how security is embedded in society.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:35.480
 My latest book is about surveillance.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:40.520
 Data and Goliath talks about what's going on in the world of surveillance and how we

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:42.900
 can regain security.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.720
 So to me all of these books are for someone who might be interested in this field because

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.400
 what they're going to do is spark interest in different directions.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.960
 They're going to give people ideas, they're going to go and research further.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.480
 And that's how you get your passion.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.480
 That's how you get your calling.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:04.140
 It's not that someone gives it to you, that you notice it going by and saying, "Hey, that's

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:05.140
 kind of neat.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:05.160
 I want to do more there."

